                                                   docs_description
                                                             <char>
1:                                                        Tap Water
2:                                                    Tap Water (T)
3:                                                Tap Water-Treated
4:                                              Tap Water Untreated
5:                 Tap Water-Treated Functioning All round the year
6: Tap Water-Treated Functioning in Summer months (April-September)

Both metadata pages carry the same caveat verbatim:
  "Variable descriptions are as listed in the original village
  directories." -- i.e. the wording is the Census's own, and SHRUG does
  not harmonise it across rounds.

WORDING DIFFERENCES THAT MATTER:
  1. TREATMENT SPLIT. 1991 and 2001 record a single undifferentiated
     'Tap Water'. 2011 splits it into TREATED and UNTREATED. Our tap11_any
     is the OR of the two, which is the only construction comparable to
     the earlier rounds; tap_treated11 has no 1991 or 2001 counterpart.
  2. AVAILABILITY vs FUNCTIONING. The 1991 and 2001 labels say only
     'Tap Water' -- availability. The 2011 base variable is also
     availability, but 2011 ADDS _allyr ('Functioning All round the year')
     and _sum ('Functioning in Summer months'), a functioning distinction
     that simply does not exist in 1991 or 2001. Our tap11_any uses the
     BASE variable, so it is the availability concept -- the comparable
     one. Switching to _allyr would break comparability.
  3. CODING. The 2011 labels state the coding explicitly, e.g.
     '(Status A(1)/NA(0))' -- 'NA' means NOT AVAILABLE, a real zero. The
     1991/2001 labels do not state their coding at all.
  4. UNIT. Neither metadata page states whether the source unit is the
     village or the habitation. This is NOT resolved by the
     documentation; a habitation-level source in one round and a
     village-level source in another would shift levels without any real
     change on the ground. Flagged, not settled.
  5. AGGREGATION TO SHRID. 1991/2001 arrive as COUNTS of villages,
     2011 as a BINARY max. Section (b3) tests whether this drives
     anything by re-running on single-village shrids only.

