------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(b5) BOTTOM LINE ON TAP COMPARABILITY
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Balanced panel, m0: tap_any = 0.1963 (1991), 0.5262 (2001), 0.4894 (2011).

  CAN TAP BE COMPARED ACROSS 1991, 2001 AND 2011 FOR THE TIMING TESTS?

  1991 vs later: NO, not at the level. 1991 tap is missing wholesale in
  whole states (section a), so the 1991 level is an artefact of which
  states happen to report. Any 1991-to-2001 change mixes a real change
  with a change in reporting coverage.

  2001 vs 2011: NO, not in the saturated states. Both rounds are
  near-complete, and the single-village restriction shows the aggregation
  difference is not driving anything. But the measured direction is a
  DECLINE over a decade in which piped coverage certainly rose, and
  15.5% of shrids nationally go 1 -> 0. Villages do not commonly lose
  a tap supply, so that flow is a measurement artefact.

  THE ARTEFACT IS IN 2001, NOT 2011. Section (b2d) shows that in
  18 states (151,877 shrids, 29% of the 2001 file) the 2001 round flags
  tap AND well AND handpump AND tubewell present in almost every shrid.
  A village cannot have every source at once: the 2001 source list is
  saturated there and pc01_vd_tap carries no information. Those same
  states are where the 'decline' sits -- they contribute 87% of it.
  The 2011 treated/untreated split is NOT the explanation: 2011 shows
  saturation in 2 state(s) against 18 in 2001.

  PRACTICAL READING: tap is usable for WITHIN-YEAR cross-sectional work,
  which is what the RD needs -- but only in states where that year's
  source list is not saturated. Do NOT read the across-census change in
  tap as a trend, and do not use 2001 at all in the saturated states.
  The timing tests need a usable-state list first, and
  output/tables/05b2e_core4_saturation_by_state.csv is that list.

  This cuts BOTH ways, and the two failures hit DIFFERENT states: 1991
  fails where tap is missing wholesale, 2001 fails where the source list
  is saturated. The per-state tables are the only safe way to pick a
  sample; no single year is usable everywhere.

